## 🔐 Relevance to Security Awareness Training
When teaching staff about the importance of updating software, "patch management" sounds like a boring IT chore. In this project, I used Nmap to demonstrate exactly what an attacker sees from the outside. I show trainees how an attacker uses banner grabbing to "read the labels" on our servers. If our server broadcasts "I am running an outdated version of Apache from 2015," the attacker knows exactly which exploit to use to break in. By visualising this reconnaissance phase, I can transform "please click update" from a nagging administrative request into a critical, common-sense defence against someone actively casing our digital perimeter. This project also developed a practical answer to one of the most common staff complaints: "Why hasn't IT fixed that problem yet?" By mapping vulnerabilities to CVSS scores adjusted for real business impact, I can show employees that security teams aren't ignoring issues — they're triaging them the same way a hospital emergency room prioritises patients. A critical vulnerability on a public-facing server gets fixed tonight. A medium-severity issue on an internal tool gets scheduled. That context turns staff frustration into cooperation. 

